      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-31T18:17:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Marked as resolved.",
      "parentUuid": "aa4b9747_e168d01c",
      "revId": "8650246985d8ec27eeb64b90415ad6ca4980a4ab",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7b0d4092_bac14f2b",
        "filename": "rust/icc/FFI.rs",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 673,
      "author": {
        "id": 4159917
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-27T19:38:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "These offsets come from untrusted `u32` fields and are converted to `usize`. On a 32-bit target, `matrix_offset` + 48 can overflow, causing a debug panic or wrapping before the later slice operation. The same pattern appears around the CLUT calculations below and in the header-size calculation. Could we use `checked_add` and bounded `get()` operations consistently, perhaps through a small reader helper? Malformed ICC data should return false rather than panic, particularly on 32-bit.",
      "revId": "8650246985d8ec27eeb64b90415ad6ca4980a4ab",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "86383bb6_8e7697b5",
        "filename": "rust/icc/FFI.rs",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 673,
      "author": {
        "id": 6035
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-28T17:48:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, the \"reader\" scheme fixes this. It now just does\n```\n  let mut mtx_reader \u003d tag.reader.sub_reader(matrix_offset)?;\n  // then 16 times it does\n  mtx_reader.read_fixed()?;\n```\n\nSo I no longer need to do any bounds checking here.",
      "parentUuid": "7b0d4092_bac14f2b",
      "revId": "8650246985d8ec27eeb64b90415ad6ca4980a4ab",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
